Addresses review feedback from #184 regarding test fixture teardown patterns and missing null message handling in the worker.

## Changes

**Test fixture teardown**: Replace single-reference teardown hooks with arrays tracking all created instances. Prevents resource leaks when factories are called multiple times in a test.

```typescript
// Before: overwrites teardown on each call
workerFactory: async ({ amqpConnectionUrl }, use) => {
  let teardownHook = () => Promise.resolve();
  await use(async (contract, handlers) => {
    const worker = await TypedAmqpWorker.create(...).resultToPromise();
    teardownHook = () => worker.close().resultToPromise(); // ⚠️ overwrites previous
    return worker;
  });
  await teardownHook();
}

// After: tracks all instances
workerFactory: async ({ amqpConnectionUrl }, use) => {
  const workers: TypedAmqpWorker<ContractDefinition>[] = [];
  await use(async (contract, handlers) => {
    const worker = await TypedAmqpWorker.create(...).resultToPromise();
    workers.push(worker);
    return worker;
  });
  await Promise.all(workers.map(w => w.close().resultToPromise()));
}
```

**Null message handling**: Add early return for null messages in worker consume callback. RabbitMQ sends `null` when cancelling consumers; previously caused crashes attempting to parse `null.content`.

```typescript
this.amqpClient.channel.consume(queue.name, async (msg) => {
  if (msg === null) {
    this.logger?.warn("Consumer cancelled by server", { consumerName, queueName });
    return;
  }
  // ... parse and validate message
});
```

The null message handling is tested through integration tests per project conventions.

